251
00:18:58,382 --> 00:19:04,340
Speaker 1: And for one, this is now one of my top three role playing systems.

252
00:19:04,540 --> 00:19:11,680
Speaker 1: Blades in the Dark gets past so many of the things that make other role playing games hard to play.

253
00:19:13,641 --> 00:19:19,700
Speaker 1: The flashback mechanisms of flashing back to a plan instead of having a plan in advance.

254
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:27,000
Speaker 1: That alone, adding something like that to almost any other role playing game, indie or not, would just literally make it better.

255
00:19:27,501 --> 00:19:36,940
Speaker 1: Like so much time gets wasted at the table with meta planning around systems instead of just doing something cool and then bringing a flashback in to figure out how you got there.

256
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:38,138
Speaker 1: Like skipping to the cool part.

257
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:47,900
Speaker 1: The dye pools, like the way the mechanics work for leveling up is just so simple and yet rich enough to give you a lot of variety in characters.

258
00:19:48,320 --> 00:20:03,580
Speaker 1: Gets past the problem we've had with systems like say Burning Wheel, where as much as I love it, it's very hard to play a game like Burning Wheel with anyone who does not know the rules as well as you do, assuming that you know the rules very well.

259
00:20:04,820 --> 00:20:18,659
Speaker 1: And the burden of playing most indie RPGs with someone who does not fully understand the rules and is not willing to learn them all, that burden is so high that I'm increasingly unwilling to do it.

260
00:20:19,241 --> 00:20:29,280
Speaker 1: And yet Blades in the Dark just works with people who don't fucking know the rules at all and the whole table supports them almost effortlessly just by the nature of the mechanics.

261
00:20:31,481 --> 00:20:40,380
Speaker 1: But the other thing that really stood out, having played all the way through to the end, is that Blades in the Dark does this balance that I think very few systems have done well.

262
00:20:41,141 --> 00:20:59,260
Speaker 1: In that it includes a setting and the setting has just enough proper nouns and numbers to make it very easy to just pull things into the game and make them work and have them all be contextual without a game master having to write a grand opus.

